course: dictionary
lesson: 15925
title: The word unpunctual
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unpunctual is a formal word, and you can work it out from its parts. The start un- means not, and punctual means on time. So unpunctual means not on time, or often late. «The bus was unpunctual and arrived twenty minutes after its timetable time.»
explain: You will meet this word in school reports and formal letters. «Ravi is often unpunctual for school.» Here it means Ravi often arrives late. At home or in the playground, people usually say «late» instead.
explain: Use the context check. Swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense. «The train was unpunctual, so we missed the start of the match.» Put in not on time and the sentence fits, so you have the right meaning.
fact: «Unpunctual» means not on time; often late.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so unpunctual is the opposite of punctual.
fact: Unpunctual is a formal word, used in reports, letters and exams; «late» is the everyday word.
fact: In maths problems about time, an unpunctual bus arrives after its timetable time.
exercise: «She is never unpunctual for her piano lessons.» What does unpunctual mean? | Very quick and speedy | Very loud | Lost in the music | *Not on time; often late
exercise: «The unpunctual bus reached the stop at 9:40, but the timetable said 9:15.» How late was the bus? | *25 minutes late | 25 minutes early | Right on time | It did not come at all
exercise: Which everyday word means the same as unpunctual? | early | *late | punctual | quiet
test: «The unpunctual guest arrived after dinner had started.» What does unpunctual mean here? | Lost | *Not on time | Hungry | Very polite
test: In the word unpunctual, what does the prefix un- mean? | very | again | *not | before
exam: «Mr Patel wrote that Sam was unpunctual three times this term.» What does this suggest? | Sam was early every day | Sam was ill all term | *Sam often arrived late | Sam won a prize for being on time
